.TH tkumdview n 0.1 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkumdview
.PP
Markdown viewer widget: a two\-pane frame with a headings outline on the left and a rendered preview on the right. The document is parsed and rendered through \fBtclutils::tumd\fR (a CommonMark subset), so the viewer needs no browser. Pure Tk/ttk; namespace \fB::tkutils::tkumdview\fR.
.SS API
.CS
set w [::tkutils::tkumdview::widget .w ?-width N? ?-height N?]   ;# -> frame path
::tkutils::tkumdview::loadFile    $w path     ;# read utf-8 file, render -> char count
::tkutils::tkumdview::setMarkdown $w md       ;# set text, rebuild outline, render -> char count
::tkutils::tkumdview::getMarkdown $w          ;# current document source
::tkutils::tkumdview::headings    $w          ;# outline of the current document
::tkutils::tkumdview::toHtml      $w          ;# HTML of the current document
::tkutils::tkumdview::tocWidget   $w          ;# path of the outline list
::tkutils::tkumdview::textWidget  $w          ;# path of the preview text widget
.CE
.SS "Widget options"
.PP
\fBwidget\fR returns the frame \fB$path\fR it creates (a \fBttk::frame\fR holding a horizontal \fBttk::panedwindow\fR: outline on the left, preview on the right). \fB\-width\fR and \fB\-height\fR (default \fB72\fR x \fB26\fR) size the preview text widget in characters and lines.
.SS "Outline and navigation"
.PP
The left pane lists the document's headings. Selecting an entry scrolls the matching heading into view in the preview, so the outline acts as a clickable table of contents. \fBtocWidget\fR and \fBtextWidget\fR return the two underlying widget paths if you need to restyle or bind them directly.
.SS "Loading and rendering"
.PP
\fBsetMarkdown\fR replaces the document, rebuilds the outline and re\-renders the preview, returning the character count. \fBloadFile\fR reads the file as utf\-8 and hands it to \fBsetMarkdown\fR, so a document renders identically under Tcl 8.6 and Tcl 9. \fBgetMarkdown\fR returns the current source.
.PP
Parsing and HTML generation are delegated to \fBtclutils::tumd\fR: \fBheadings\fR and \fBtoHtml\fR are convenience pass\-throughs that run the engine over the current document, so the viewer and any export stay consistent.
.CS
package require tkutils::tkumdview
set w [::tkutils::tkumdview::widget .v]
pack $w -fill both -expand 1
::tkutils::tkumdview::loadFile $w README.md
set html [::tkutils::tkumdview::toHtml $w]   ;# same render, as HTML
.CE
